<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<chapter xml:id="cfr-cubic-atoms">
  <title>The atom of a cubic fourfold</title>
  <section xml:id="cfr-cubic-spectrum">
    <title>The quantum spectrum</title>
    <p>Let <m>X\subset\mathbb P^5</m> be a smooth cubic fourfold and let <m>h</m> be its hyperplane class. Its total cohomology has dimension <m>27</m>. The five-dimensional ambient part is spanned by <m>1,h,h^2,h^3,h^4</m>, and its orthogonal complement is the primitive middle cohomology of dimension <m>22</m>. The primitive Hodge numbers are
    <md>(h^{3,1}_{\mathrm{prim}},h^{2,2}_{\mathrm{prim}},h^{1,3}_{\mathrm{prim}})=(1,20,1).</md>
    A cubic is Noether–Lefschetz general when its rational Hodge classes are exactly the ambient classes. Such cubics form the complement of the countable union of Hassett divisors.</p>
    <p>The quantum multiplication matrix can be obtained from the quantum cohomology of complete intersections <xref ref="bib-cfr-beauville-quantum" detail="Theorem"/>. In the basis just specified, small quantum multiplication by <m>c_1(TX)=3h</m> is
    <md>K(q)=3\begin{pmatrix}0&amp;0&amp;6q&amp;0&amp;0\\1&amp;0&amp;0&amp;15q&amp;0\\0&amp;1&amp;0&amp;0&amp;6q\\0&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0&amp;0\\0&amp;0&amp;0&amp;1&amp;0\end{pmatrix}.</md>
    Its characteristic polynomial is
    <md>\det(\lambda I-K(q))=\lambda^2(\lambda^3-729q).</md>
    At the algebraic specialization <m>q=1</m>, the nonzero eigenvalues are <m>9,9\zeta,9\zeta^2</m>, where <m>\zeta^3=1</m> and <m>\zeta\ne1</m>. Each is simple. The zero eigenvalue has one Jordan block of size two. The matrix and its non-archimedean interpretation are given in <xref ref="bib-cfr-kkpy" detail="Example 6.6(iii) and the proof of Theorem 6.8"/>.</p>
    <p>On primitive cohomology, small multiplication by <m>h</m> is zero. This can be seen directly from monodromy and grading: the primitive representation is irreducible, so a monodromy-equivariant endomorphism is scalar there; a nonzero term of multiplication by <m>h</m> preserving middle degree would require a curve class of anticanonical degree one, whereas these degrees are multiples of three. The full zero generalized eigenspace consequently has dimension <m>24</m>.</p>
    <p>At <m>q=1</m>, the two ambient directions in the zero generalized eigenspace are spanned by <m>w_3=h^3-21</m> and <m>w_4=h^4-6h</m>. They satisfy <m>K(1)w_3=3w_4</m> and <m>K(1)w_4=0</m>. Their summands have different cohomological degrees, but all have Hochschild degree zero. This is compatible with the Tate-folded Hodge convention.</p>
    <p>For a very general cubic, the zero part persists as a single Hodge atom. Its representation consists of primitive cohomology and these two ambient directions. The atomic multiset has three one-dimensional point atoms and one atom <m>\alpha_X</m> of dimension <m>24</m>. The atom satisfies <m>\rho_{\alpha_X}=2</m> and has Hodge polynomial
    <md>P_{\alpha_X}(t)=t^2+22+t^{-2}.</md>
    This is <xref ref="bib-cfr-kkpy" detail="Corollary 6.12"/>. The assertion concerns the decomposition over the locus of Hodge parameters. The spectrum of one specialized matrix alone would not establish it.</p>
  </section>
  <section xml:id="cfr-cubic-irrationality">
    <title>The surface obstruction</title>
    <p>Points and curves have no classes of Hochschild degree two. Suppose that an atom with a nonzero degree-two part comes from a projective surface. Passing to a minimal model removes only point atoms. A minimal surface with <m>p_g&gt;0</m> has nef canonical class. The virtual dimension constraint on Gromov–Witten invariants forces its Euler operator to have a single eigenvalue, so its total cohomology is one atom.</p>
    <p>That surface atom contains the unit, an ample divisor class, and the point class. These give three independent rational Hodge classes. Every atom from a surface with a nonzero degree-two contribution must therefore satisfy <m>\rho\geq3</m>. The obstruction is the incompatibility of a holomorphic two-form with only two Hodge classes in the same atom.</p>
    <theorem xml:id="cfr-very-general-irrational">
      <statement><p>A very general smooth complex cubic fourfold is irrational.</p></statement>
      <proof><p>The atom <m>\alpha_X</m> has a degree-two part of dimension one and <m>\rho_{\alpha_X}=2</m>. It occurs on no point, curve, or surface. The obstruction in <xref ref="cfr-atomic-obstruction"/> applies in dimension four. This is the argument of <xref ref="bib-cfr-kkpy" detail="Theorem 6.8"/>.</p></proof>
    </theorem>
    <p>Ordinary cohomology does not retain the condition that the period line and the two Hodge classes belong to one quantum summand. A decomposition into Hodge substructures can split the cohomology of a surface and distribute its algebraic classes among other summands. The atom condition prevents this for a minimal surface with a holomorphic two-form. This is why replacing the atom by primitive cohomology alone loses the numerical obstruction.</p>
    <p>Special cubics have additional rational Hodge classes. The degree-two part of their zero sector remains, but its Hodge-class count can increase. The inequality <m>\rho\leq2</m> used in this proof no longer applies. The irrationality of very general cubics consequently leaves a separate problem on each Hassett divisor.</p>
  </section>
</chapter>
